# Auth Backend – Full Documentation

This backend is a JWT-based authentication and user management service built on **FastAPI**, **SQLAlchemy (async)** and **JWT**.

It exposes REST endpoints for:

- User registration
- Login
- Logout
- Access token refresh
- Token validation (access + refresh)
- Health check
- Basic user info

and provides a Python Authentication API (`auth.api.Authentification`) for:

- Verifying tokens
- Extracting user information from tokens
- Protecting FastAPI routes via decorators/dependencies
- Configuring JWT and database parameters

The code is organized as a Python package:

```text
auth/
  backend/
    main.py
    schemas.py
  api/
    Authentification.py
    security.py
    db.py
    db_models.py
    config.py


⸻

1. Configuration

1.1 Environment Variables

The service can be configured via environment variables:

# JWT configuration
JWT_SECRET                 # secret key for signing JWTs (required in production)
JWT_ALG                    # algorithm, default "HS256"
ACCESS_TOKEN_MINUTES       # access token lifetime in minutes, default "15"
REFRESH_TOKEN_DAYS         # refresh token lifetime in days, default "14"

# Database (SQLAlchemy async)
DATABASE_URL               # e.g. "postgresql+asyncpg://user:pass@auth_db:5432/appdb"
DATABASE_USER              # optional, informational
DATABASE_PASSWORD          # optional, informational
DATABASE_TABLE             # optional, informational

DB_POOL_SIZE               # connection pool size, default "10"
DB_MAX_OVERFLOW            # max overflow connections, default "20"
DB_POOL_TIMEOUT            # pool timeout in seconds, default "30"
DB_POOL_RECYCLE            # recycle connections in seconds, default "1800"

# App behavior
ENV                        # "dev" or "prod"
DB_CREATE_TABLES           # "1" to auto-create tables on startup (for dev)
LOG_LEVEL                  # logging level, e.g. "INFO", "DEBUG"
CORS_ORIGINS               # comma-separated list for CORS
TRUSTED_HOSTS              # comma-separated list for trusted hosts

1.2 Runtime Configuration (Python)

Configuration is exposed via auth.api.Authentification and backed by auth.api.config.

from auth.api.Authentification import (
    set_database_url,
    set_database_user,
    set_database_password,
    set_database_table,
    set_secret,
)

# Example: configure the authentication library at startup
set_database_url("postgresql+asyncpg://user:pass@auth_db:5432/appdb")
set_database_user("db_user")
set_database_password("db_password")
set_database_table("users")
set_secret("YOUR_JWT_SECRET")


⸻

2. Token Model (JWT)

Two JWT token types are used: refresh and access.

2.1 Refresh Token

Longer-lived token used only to obtain new access tokens and to drive session management.

Typical payload:

{
  "sub": "123",          // user ID
  "admin": true,         // admin flag, may be used for optional checks
  "typ": "refresh",      // token type
  "jti": "uuid-string",  // unique token ID
  "iat": 1732051200,     // issued at (unix timestamp)
  "exp": 1734653200      // expires at (unix timestamp)
}

The refresh token itself is not used for authorization on protected business endpoints.

2.2 Access Token

Short-lived token used for all authenticated API access.

Typical payload:

{
  "sub": "123",          // user ID
  "admin": true,         // admin flag
  "typ": "access",
  "iat": 1732051200,     // issued at (unix timestamp)
  "exp": 1732054800      // expires at (unix timestamp)
}

	•	sub is the user ID.
	•	admin controls admin-only access